{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 542 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "da7906cd4531c4eed9429dffda06140458ca226ee0066917e36ab63476397787" :edb/db-before-t 541 :edb/db-after-t 542 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533854 50 #inst "2026-10-05T03:57:32.633Z" 13194139533854 true] [17592186096723 1020 #edb/ref 17592186255618 13194139533854 true] [17592186096724 1020 #edb/ref 17592186255625 13194139533854 true] [17592186096725 1020 #edb/ref 17592186255632 13194139533854 true] [17592186096725 1020 #edb/ref 17592186255639 13194139533854 true] [17592186096726 1020 #edb/ref 17592186255643 13194139533854 true] [17592186255618 1017 #edb/ref 17592186255619 13194139533854 true] [17592186255618 1089 "q-104782" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255618 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533854 true] [17592186255618 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947756/assets/2a11d5b84bdf66b6406f9465ba6a8bc8ecdf364bea2dcd6902034990a74ef0a9.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is given above. Find ${f}^{′}(-3).$" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255618 1113 #uuid "37afa10e-5c67-5b1e-ad86-c44cc76a4131" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255618 1145 "To compute ${f}^{′}(-3),$ we need to find the slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ at $x=-3.$\n\nLet's consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ on $[-5,-2].$ Notice that $x=-3$ lies in this interval.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947756/assets/59159512fa1b51477f171584a3717e2bf2774e8c940b59525fe478e1bcf6aece.png)\n\nThe slope of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in (-5,-2)$ is constant and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{1-4}{-2-(-5)}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(-3)=-1.$" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255619 1000 #edb/ref 17592186255620 13194139533854 true] [17592186255619 1000 #edb/ref 17592186255621 13194139533854 true] [17592186255619 1000 #edb/ref 17592186255622 13194139533854 true] [17592186255619 1000 #edb/ref 17592186255623 13194139533854 true] [17592186255619 1000 #edb/ref 17592186255624 13194139533854 true] [17592186255619 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533854 true] [17592186255619 1041 "selection" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255619 1104 #edb/ref 17592186255623 13194139533854 true] [17592186255619 1110 #uuid "0962a068-c807-584e-9232-cf2c4b1afafc" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255620 1034 "-\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255620 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533854 true] [17592186255620 1111 #uuid "c62f9f05-3fba-546a-9ed3-c63c34d8c984" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255621 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255621 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533854 true] [17592186255621 1111 #uuid "4a8ea702-a177-50e0-9663-391e3ce7c672" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255622 1034 "1" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255622 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533854 true] [17592186255622 1111 #uuid "86cae644-34bf-59b9-99b2-d6b752676a75" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255623 1034 "-1" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255623 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533854 true] [17592186255623 1111 #uuid "cd250dfa-ca60-54a2-ab12-40e2e658bfa3" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255624 1034 "5" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255624 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533854 true] [17592186255624 1111 #uuid "77bd1567-ec27-5dc4-be74-7001e937903a" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255625 1017 #edb/ref 17592186255626 13194139533854 true] [17592186255625 1089 "q-104784" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255625 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533854 true] [17592186255625 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947756/assets/e5bcb5723747beb905cc93e3bcf8e3a5be7bf566cf408998d3173490bd2486b5.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(-3)$ where $h(x)=-3f(x)-g(x).$" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255625 1113 #uuid "6b828410-633b-56b5-9708-af2c0412ad03" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255625 1145 "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=-3{f}^{′}(x)-{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-3)=-3{f}^{′}(-3)-{g}^{′}(-3).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=-3$ corresponds to\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-5,-2)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-5,-1)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947756/assets/4f8b5f4c3a87f6cb1c346a4672df96a068d74690f0b7c9c6f9a77a9a3710a34a.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-5,-2)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{-1-3}{-2-(-5)}=-\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(-3)=-\\frac{4}{3}.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-5,-1)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{4-4}{-1-(-5)}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(-3)=0.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-3) & =-3{f}^{′}(-3)-{g}^{′}(-3) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3})-0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255626 1000 #edb/ref 17592186255627 13194139533854 true] [17592186255626 1000 #edb/ref 17592186255628 13194139533854 true] [17592186255626 1000 #edb/ref 17592186255629 13194139533854 true] [17592186255626 1000 #edb/ref 17592186255630 13194139533854 true] [17592186255626 1000 #edb/ref 17592186255631 13194139533854 true] [17592186255626 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533854 true] [17592186255626 1041 "selection" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255626 1104 #edb/ref 17592186255629 13194139533854 true] [17592186255626 1110 #uuid "a2d43708-64ed-5a56-9c46-95bab92ffbd7" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255627 1034 "1" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255627 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533854 true] [17592186255627 1111 #uuid "a692e996-3a60-5f66-89e1-9e59d2bc47a3" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255628 1034 "-4" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255628 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533854 true] [17592186255628 1111 #uuid "48bbff25-f78c-5cf6-9959-bc6e0902e482" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255629 1034 "4" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255629 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533854 true] [17592186255629 1111 #uuid "de431da7-a3de-5409-a626-2ffcc79dc041" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255630 1034 "0" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255630 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533854 true] [17592186255630 1111 #uuid "3101713c-3ec7-5808-ac92-41fa98fb9710" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255631 1034 "-1" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255631 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533854 true] [17592186255631 1111 #uuid "e7371afe-5750-5a7e-b179-e5701f8fbfba" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255632 1017 #edb/ref 17592186255633 13194139533854 true] [17592186255632 1089 "q-104795" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255632 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533854 true] [17592186255632 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947756/assets/61c9cb5fed816e028cf47e3d6b264a40a2fc4c04bc5d330e8662a8dfd8fa26e1.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(2)$ where $h(x)=f(x)g(x).$" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255632 1113 #uuid "4daf08f1-ddc5-57c5-bb35-3b3c140a8cd6" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255632 1145 "The product rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)+f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(2)={f}^{′}(2)\\cdot g(2)+f(2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(2).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(2)=4$ and $g(2)=3.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-2,4)$ equals $0.$ So, ${f}^{′}(2)=0.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (-4,4)$ equals $\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, ${g}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(2) & ={f}^{′}(2)\\cdot g(2)+f(2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(2) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 3+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255633 1000 #edb/ref 17592186255634 13194139533854 true] [17592186255633 1000 #edb/ref 17592186255635 13194139533854 true] [17592186255633 1000 #edb/ref 17592186255636 13194139533854 true] [17592186255633 1000 #edb/ref 17592186255637 13194139533854 true] [17592186255633 1000 #edb/ref 17592186255638 13194139533854 true] [17592186255633 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533854 true] [17592186255633 1041 "selection" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255633 1104 #edb/ref 17592186255635 13194139533854 true] [17592186255633 1110 #uuid "770d388c-a58a-57b6-ae7b-66a20b7e87ca" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255634 1034 "3" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255634 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533854 true] [17592186255634 1111 #uuid "294c6ef3-3e63-52f0-958a-96a5c1c1347c" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255635 1034 "2" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255635 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533854 true] [17592186255635 1111 #uuid "5c9076bc-d400-5517-a80c-b0deb326ae89" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255636 1034 "-3" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255636 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533854 true] [17592186255636 1111 #uuid "47900e39-52af-5f9e-a131-9a7553dac10c" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255637 1034 "5" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255637 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533854 true] [17592186255637 1111 #uuid "3ee6ba69-ae86-5147-b5fe-437b9bc6290a" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255638 1034 "-2" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255638 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533854 true] [17592186255638 1111 #uuid "3d5a7c40-1962-5ef0-944d-1d3a9087c033" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255639 1017 #edb/ref 17592186255640 13194139533854 true] [17592186255639 1089 "q-344894" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255639 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533854 true] [17592186255639 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947756/assets/bd78704be61e30879d4e659a255f93f5c8b6e560951d06b23cf547c8846b01ee.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=h(x)$ are given above. Find ${p}^{′}(2)$ where $p(x)=f(x)h(x).$\n\n${p}^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255639 1113 #uuid "15020dfc-75e9-546a-bdc3-2e8b45dfd09e" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255639 1145 "The product rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{p}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)\\cdot h(x)+f(x)\\cdot {h}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{p}^{′}(2)={f}^{′}(2)\\cdot h(2)+f(2)\\cdot {h}^{′}(2).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(2)=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $h(2)=0.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (1,3)$ equals $\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, ${f}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The slope of $h(x)$ on $x\\in (1,3)$ equals $2.$ So, ${h}^{′}(2)=2.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{′}(2) & ={f}^{′}(2)\\cdot h(2)+f(2)\\cdot {h}^{′}(2) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot 0+(\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255640 1000 #edb/ref 17592186255641 13194139533854 true] [17592186255640 1000 #edb/ref 17592186255642 13194139533854 true] [17592186255640 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533854 true] [17592186255640 1041 "field-1" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255640 1104 #edb/ref 17592186255641 13194139533854 true] [17592186255640 1110 #uuid "35629b96-66b9-51f6-8933-3f9798eb86c1" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255641 1034 "3" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255641 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533854 true] [17592186255641 1111 #uuid "805cb65d-8a23-5f86-a277-a5c8b0aa2eae" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255642 1034 "2" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255642 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533854 true] [17592186255642 1111 #uuid "238add62-4d3d-5bb2-b247-fbbd656e9249" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255643 1017 #edb/ref 17592186255644 13194139533854 true] [17592186255643 1089 "q-344914" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255643 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533854 true] [17592186255643 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947756/assets/f23d8acd8b59e630987fd9a04b71a5e0813f5c084f22489af342ba04c34eb003.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find ${h}^{′}(-1)$ where $h(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$\n\n${h}^{′}(-1)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255643 1113 #uuid "513f7389-d968-58ce-94d6-fe04861c05c4" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255643 1145 "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-1)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(-1)\\cdot g(-1)-f(-1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-1)}{(g(-1))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(-1)=1$ and $g(-1)=2.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-3,1)$ equals $1.$ So, ${f}^{′}(-1)=1.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (-3,1)$ equals $\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, ${g}^{′}(-1)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-1) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(-1)\\cdot g(-1)-f(-1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-1)}{(g(-1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})}{(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{3}{2}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255644 1000 #edb/ref 17592186255645 13194139533854 true] [17592186255644 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533854 true] [17592186255644 1041 "field-1" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255644 1104 #edb/ref 17592186255645 13194139533854 true] [17592186255644 1110 #uuid "012c26aa-6f05-5865-a302-36f99157d46c" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255645 1034 "\\frac{3}{8}" 13194139533854 true] [17592186255645 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533854 true] [17592186255645 1111 #uuid "18b53151-7834-5a3c-872e-ece384db61d4" 13194139533854 true]] :edb/tempids {"q-104782" 17592186255618 "q-104782/selection" 17592186255619 "q-104782/selection/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" 17592186255620 "q-104782/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" 17592186255621 "q-104782/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" 17592186255622 "q-104782/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" 17592186255623 "q-104782/se